        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/b59d3dc8-f089-48bb-8fed-ec043b8182bd,1774040710731/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C312%2C6000%2C3375%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='Two men in suits shaking hands.' width='620' height='349' title='Prime Minister Mark Carney meets with French President Emmanuel Macron as they attend the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland on Tuesday, Jan. 20, 2026.'/><p>As European allies quietly explore France's nuclear umbrella amid doubts about U.S. reliability, Canada faces an uncomfortable question: join or stay clear. While Ottawa rejects going nuclear, extending French deterrence to North America risks inflaming tensions with Washington \u2014 and raises stark questions about who would defend whom in a crisis.</p>",
