        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/6f7b46c1-484c-41fa-907e-5cdf93e49146,1774039804157/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C281%2C5388%2C3030%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='FILE - The sun rises over fishing boats in the Atlantic Ocean, Sept. 8, 2022, off of Kennebunkport, Maine.' width='620' height='349' title='FILE - The sun rises over fishing boats in the Atlantic Ocean, Sept. 8, 2022, off of Kennebunkport, Maine. The United Nations body that regulates the world\u2019s ocean floor is preparing to resume negotiations in July 2023, that could open the international seabed for mining, including for materials vital for the green energy transition. (AP Photo/Robert F. Bukaty, File)'/><p>Even as the race to mine the ocean floor for important minerals intensifies, a UN-body tasked with establishing regulations for the emerging industry ended a key global meeting this week without a new mining code.</p>",
